<article class="env-remark" id="05PQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05PQ">Remark <span data-tag="05PQ">19.9.4</span>.</a> If $\mathcal{A}$ is a “big” abelian category, i.e., if $\mathcal{A}$ has a class of objects, then Lemma <a href="/tag/05PN" data-tag="05PN">19.9.2</a> does not work. In this case, given any set of objects $E \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{A})$ there exists an abelian full subcategory $\mathcal{A}' \subset \mathcal{A}$ such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{A}')$ is a set and $E \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{A}')$. Then one can apply Lemma <a href="/tag/05PN" data-tag="05PN">19.9.2</a> to $\mathcal{A}'$. One can use this to prove that results depending on a diagram chase hold in $\mathcal{A}$. </p>
</article>